            <div xml:id="d584">

                <pb facs="0658" n="556" xml:id="pg556"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/12605: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China (Johnson) to the Acting
                        Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1933-12-30T12:00:00+08:00">December 30,
                        1933—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received December 30—4:40 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>940. Reference Legation’s telegram 931, December 21 [<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >26</hi>], 6 [<hi rendition="#italic">1</hi>] p.m., the Vice Consul at
                    Foochow reports December 29, 3 p.m., that no further air raids have occurred;
                    that British vessel <hi rendition="#italic">Verity</hi> refused to allow the
                    Nineteenth Route Army to search British merchant vessel <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Hatching</hi> placing armed guard on board; that northward movement of
                    troops of Nineteenth Route Army apparently continues; that, according to
                    reliable sources, troops are still friendly to foreigners; and that no open
                    break among leaders in the regime is yet noticeable.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
